  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Why Does My Newly Painted Door Look Streaky?",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
    "url": "http://coloradoremodeling.company"
  },
  "articleBody": "It's a common frustration to finish painting a door only to step back and see unsightly streaks or brush marks. There are a few reasons this can happen, and understanding them can help you avoid it next time. One major culprit is applying the paint too thickly or not maintaining a wet edge. When paint starts to dry too quickly before you can smooth it out, streaks appear. Using the wrong type of brush or roller cover can also contribute; cheap tools often leave undesirable textures. Sometimes, it's about the paint itself – a lower quality paint might not self-level as well as a premium product designed for smooth finishes. Not cleaning and lightly sanding the door before painting is another big one, as any dust or imperfections will show right through the new coat. And don't forget about drying times! If you rush and apply a second coat before the first is properly dry, you can reactivate the underlying paint, leading to an uneven finish. For a truly smooth, factory-like finish, especially on high-traffic doors, professional painters often use sprayers or specific techniques that are hard to replicate at home. If you're aiming for perfection on your interior doors, HH Remodeling LLC has the expertise and tools to prevent these streaky headaches, ensuring a beautiful, smooth result every time.",
